  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
          "text": "Mount Everest, located in Nepal and Tibet, is usually said to be the highest mountain on Earth. ",
          "start": 227,
          "end": 323,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "Mount Everest, located in Nepal and Tibet, is usually said to be the highest mountain on Earth."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-11",
          "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
          "text": "You may be surprised to learn that Everest is not the tallest mountain on Earth, either. ",
          "start": 1153,
          "end": 1242,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-10",
              "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
              "text": "That makes Chimborazo the closest point on Earth to the stars.\n",
              "start": 1090,
              "end": 1153,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-12",
              "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
              "text": "That honor belongs to Mauna Kea, a volcano on the Big Island of Hawaii. ",
              "start": 1242,
              "end": 1314,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "That makes Chimborazo the closest point on Earth to the stars. You may be surprised to learn that Everest is not the tallest mountain on Earth, either. That honor belongs to Mauna Kea, a volcano on the Big Island of Hawaii."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
          "text": "Reaching 8,848 meters at its summit, Everest is indeed the highest point above global mean sea level—the average level for the ocean surface from which elevations are measured. ",
          "start": 323,
          "end": 500,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-3",
              "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
              "text": "Mount Everest, located in Nepal and Tibet, is usually said to be the highest mountain on Earth. ",
              "start": 227,
              "end": 323,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-f79bf0925486::sentence-5",
              "documentId": "doc-f79bf0925486",
              "text": "But the summit of Mt. Everest is not the farthest point from Earth’s center.\n",
              "start": 500,
              "end": 577,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Mount Everest, located in Nepal and Tibet, is usually said to be the highest mountain on Earth. Reaching 8,848 meters at its summit, Everest is indeed the highest point above global mean sea level—the average level for the ocean surface from which elevations are measured. But the summit of Mt. Everest is not the farthest point from Earth’s center."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-e42dc78c7afa::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-e42dc78c7afa",
          "text": "planet, growing to an elevation of 4,170 m (13,681 feet) above sea level with an additional depth of 9,144 m (30,000 feet) below sea level, which makes this shield volcano taller from base to top than Mount Everest. ",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 216,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "planet, growing to an elevation of 4,170 m (13,681 feet) above sea level with an additional depth of 9,144 m (30,000 feet) below sea level, which makes this shield volcano taller from base to top than Mount Everest."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-e42dc78c7afa::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-e42dc78c7afa",
          "text": "The accretion, or deposition, of highly fluid lava flows that we see on Mauna Loa can spread over great distances and eventually cool as thin, low-angle dipping layers away from the summit. ",
          "start": 762,
          "end": 952,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-e42dc78c7afa::sentence-5",
              "documentId": "doc-e42dc78c7afa",
              "text": "There are two effusive eruption subcategories that geoscientists use to describe lava flow type, pāhoehoe and ‘A‘ā. ",
              "start": 646,
              "end": 762,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "There are two effusive eruption subcategories that geoscientists use to describe lava flow type, pāhoehoe and ‘A‘ā. The accretion, or deposition, of highly fluid lava flows that we see on Mauna Loa can spread over great distances and eventually cool as thin, low-angle dipping layers away from the summit."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-e42dc78c7afa::sentence-2",
          "documentId": "doc-e42dc78c7afa",
          "text": "Mauna Loa makes up roughly 51% of the land mass on the Big Island, with a weight so heavy that it physically depresses the ocean floor beneath it. ",
          "start": 216,
          "end": 363,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-e42dc78c7afa::sentence-1",
              "documentId": "doc-e42dc78c7afa",
              "text": "planet, growing to an elevation of 4,170 m (13,681 feet) above sea level with an additional depth of 9,144 m (30,000 feet) below sea level, which makes this shield volcano taller from base to top than Mount Everest. ",
              "start": 0,
              "end": 216,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-e42dc78c7afa::sentence-3",
              "documentId": "doc-e42dc78c7afa",
              "text": "It is a shield volcano, which is a broad, gently sloping dome-like structure built almost entirely from fluid lava flows pouring out of a central vent(s). ",
              "start": 363,
              "end": 518,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "planet, growing to an elevation of 4,170 m (13,681 feet) above sea level with an additional depth of 9,144 m (30,000 feet) below sea level, which makes this shield volcano taller from base to top than Mount Everest. Mauna Loa makes up roughly 51% of the land mass on the Big Island, with a weight so heavy that it physically depresses the ocean floor beneath it. It is a shield volcano, which is a broad, gently sloping dome-like structure built almost entirely from fluid lava flows pouring out of a central vent(s)."
        }
      ]
    }
